    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "When coaches emphasized self- and norm-referenced definitions of success and provided opportunities for decision making, gymnasts reported greater physical competence 7 months later. In turn, higher perceived ability predicted more adaptive well-being over time, including higher self-esteem and lower disordered eating."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "The study included a purposeful sample of competitive gymnasts, so the results may not generalize to other youth sport contexts. Additionally, the performance climate scale only tapped the unequal recognition aspect, and participants reported relatively low levels of performance climate."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Future research should continue to consider indices of physical, social, emotional, and cognitive maturity with youth samples to control for developmental differences in well-being outcomes. More theory-driven longitudinal studies can determine relationships and mediation over time and inform interventions to promote well-being among youth participants."
